course: history
lesson: 63
title: Writing, laws and money: how strangers learned to trust each other
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Jack's friend says writing was invented in one place and everyone copied the ABC. Wren's friend adds that peoples without writing had no laws, just chaos. Both are wrong, and Jack answers «nah, bro».
explain: What really happened: writing was invented on its own at least three times, in Mesopotamia, in China and in Mexico and Central America. And many peoples kept laws in memory, speeches and objects, like the Haudenosaunee Great Law of Peace, remembered with belts of shell beads called wampum.
explain: Think of lending a friend a sticker: you trust them because you know them. With strangers you need more: written promises, laws everyone can see, fair weights on the scales, and money that both sides accept.
explain: Money did not have to be coins. People paid in grain, silver weighed on scales and cowrie shells. Jayden's aunt says the school story of «first barter, then money» is «lowkey contested», which means scholars quietly but really disagree about it.
explain: Ancient people moaned like us too. A clay tablet from Ur, about 3,750 years old, is a customer's angry letter about bad copper. When Liam asks who made the first coin, his Geordie gran says «ask Google, pet»: pet is how people in north-east England say love or dear.
fact: Writing was invented separately in Mesopotamia about 5,200 years ago, in China over 3,000 years ago, and in Mexico and Central America over 2,000 years ago; whether Egypt's writing was separate is debated.
fact: Around 1820 only about one adult in eight in the world could read, mostly priests, scribes, merchants and nobles; today nearly nine in ten can.
fact: King Hammurabi of Babylon had about 280 laws carved on a tall black stone about 3,800 years ago; the stone is now in the Louvre museum in Paris; «eye for an eye» applied between equals, while a rich man who hurt a poorer person often paid silver instead.
fact: The story that people first swapped goods and then invented money is contested: scholars have found few, if any, communities that lived by swapping alone, and neighbours more often lent, gave and remembered debts.
fact: Cowrie shells were used as money for hundreds of years in parts of Africa, India and China; in early Mesopotamia people paid in grain or silver weighed on scales.
fact: The first known coins were made in Lydia, in what is now Turkey, around 600 BCE; coins appeared in China and India around the same time.
fact: A ruler's stamp on a coin promised its weight, so traders did not need to weigh metal every time.
fact: Paper money spread in China about 1,000 years ago and in Europe in the 1600s; today most money is numbers in bank computers.
fact: A clay tablet from Ur, about 3,750 years old, holds a letter from a man called Nanni complaining to the trader Ea-nasir about poor copper and rude treatment.
fact: Kings and cities set standard weights and measures so buyers were not cheated; France created the metric system in the 1790s, and most of the world now uses it.
fact: Many peoples kept laws without writing: the Haudenosaunee Great Law of Peace was remembered with wampum belts, and Somali clans used xeer, a law kept by elders.
exercise: How many times, at least, was writing invented separately? | At least three times | Only once, in Egypt | Exactly ten times, all on the same day | Never; it was always there
exercise: Where was writing invented about 5,200 years ago? | In Mesopotamia | In southern Britain | In the deserts of Australia | In the Amazon forest of Brazil
exercise: Around 1820, how many adults in the world could read? | About one in eight | Nearly all of them | About nine in ten | Exactly half of them
exercise: Who usually could read for most of history? | Priests, scribes, merchants and nobles | Every farmer and every child in the village | Only kings, and nobody else in the land | Nobody at all until the first printed books
exercise: Whose laws are carved on the tall black stone in the Louvre? | King Hammurabi of Babylon | Julius Caesar of Rome | King John of England | Confucius of ancient China
exercise: Were Hammurabi's laws equal for everyone? | No, the rich often paid silver instead | Yes, exactly equal for all, rich or poor | Yes, and slaves even had more rights | No, only children had to obey them
exercise: What do many scholars say about the barter story? | It is contested and may be too simple | It is proven beyond any doubt by old coins | Barter was only invented after coins | It was made up by modern computer games
exercise: Where were cowrie shells used as money? | In parts of Africa, India and China | Only in Antarctica | Only in Rome | Nowhere at all
exercise: How did people in early Mesopotamia often pay? | In grain or weighed silver | With printed paper notes | With bank cards and phones | With cowrie shells only
exercise: Where were the first known coins made? | In Lydia, in what is now Turkey | In London, under the Romans | In Egypt, for the pyramid builders | In Iceland, by the Vikings
exercise: Why did a ruler stamp a coin? | To promise its weight, so traders need not weigh it | To make it heavier, so it would be worth more | To make it magic, so it would bring good luck | To stop people spending it outside his own land
exercise: Where did paper money first spread? | In China, about 1,000 years ago | In Europe, 5,000 years ago | In Egypt, in the time of the pyramids | In America, in 1990
exercise: What did Nanni complain about to Ea-nasir? | Poor copper and rude treatment | A late delivery of bread and beer | A broken chariot wheel he had bought | Too much homework from his teacher
exercise: Who created the metric system in the 1790s? | France | China, under its emperors | The Roman Empire | The kings of Iceland
exercise: How did the Haudenosaunee remember their Great Law of Peace? | With wampum belts, speeches and memory | With a long book printed in Paris | On a tall black stone like Hammurabi's | It was never remembered or passed on
exercise: What is xeer? | A customary law of Somali clans, kept by elders | A kind of coin from Lydia | A clay tablet from Ur | A French unit of weight
test: Jack's mate says everyone copied the ABC from one place. Nah, bro? | Nah, several peoples invented writing on their own | Nah, he's right, writing came only from Rome | Yeah, true, China copied Egypt's ABC thousands of years ago | Nah, writing was only invented around 1800
test: «most ppl couldnt read back then right?» asks Isla. What is right? | Right; around 1820 only about one adult in eight could | No, nearly everyone could read, even in the villages | No, reading was banned for everyone except kings | Right, and still nobody can read today
test: Omar says Hammurabi's eye for an eye was equal for all. What would the guide add? | It was written down but not equal; the rich often paid silver | Yes, slaves and nobles had exactly the same rights in court | It was really a law about eye doctors and their fees | Hammurabi had no laws at all; the stone is just a story
test: Jayden's teacher says money came from barter; his aunt says lowkey contested. What does she mean? | Scholars really disagree; debts and gifts may be older than swapping | She agrees completely: everyone knows barter came first, then coins, then banks | Money came from aliens who visited Mesopotamia long ago | Barter was only invented last year, on the internet
test: Priya laughs: shells as money? What makes anything work as money? | People trust and accept it | It must be made of gold | It must be printed on paper | It must have a king's face
test: Charlie: «is money even real any more?» His dad pays by phone. What is true? | Money keeps changing shape, but it always ran on trust | Money disappeared in 2000 and only numbers are left | Phones make new money from nothing every time you pay | Only metal coins are real money; the rest is pretend
test: Grace reads the oldest customer complaint is about bad copper. legit or nah? | Legit; Nanni wrote it to Ea-nasir on clay | Nah, ancient people never complained about stuff | Nah, it's about a broken phone, not copper | Legit, but it was written in 1990 as a joke
test: What would happen at a market if every stall used different weights? | Buyers could be cheated and trust would fall | Everything at the market would be free | Apples would grow bigger to fill the weights | Nothing at all, because nobody would notice
test: Wren's friend says peoples without writing had no laws, just chaos. What would Wren say? | Not true; laws were kept in memory, speeches and wampum | True, laws can only exist if they are written on paper | True, they had no leaders, so nobody made any rules | Not true; they kept their laws on early computers
test: In your game you get a «mint» building that stamps coins. What did a real ruler's stamp do? | It promised the coin's weight to traders | It made the coin worth nothing at all | It made the coin magic in the king's eyes | It turned the silver inside into pure gold
test: what if no one could ever write anything down. what would we lose | Records, letters and laws that outlive the people who made them | Nothing, because people's memory is always perfect anyway, so who needs it | Only maths homework, and we would all be happier | The ability to talk, since speech comes from writing
exam: Identify the region in which writing first appeared, approximately five thousand two hundred years ago. | Mesopotamia | Scandinavia, among the Vikings | The interior of Australia | The southern part of South America
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the laws of Hammurabi. | They were inscribed publicly but did not treat all ranks equally | They conferred identical rights upon all persons, free or enslaved | They were preserved solely in oral form by the priests | They were enacted in the nineteenth century in France
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the earliest known coinage? | It was minted in Lydia circa six hundred BCE | It was minted in France during the seventeen-nineties | It was printed on paper in Rome | It consisted of plastic cards in Mesopotamia
exam: Ascertain the manner in which the Haudenosaunee Great Law of Peace was preserved. | By memory, oratory and wampum belts | By a carved stone in Babylon | By printed books kept in Paris and London | It was never preserved in any form
